## Development Guidelines

### Scraping Guidelines
- Implement rate limiting and delays between requests
- Handle network errors gracefully
- Cache responses when appropriate
- Follow robots.txt guidelines
- Log all scraping activities
- Validate scraped data against schemas
- Handle different academic year formats

### Data Processing
- Clean and normalize data before storage
- Implement data validation
- Handle edge cases and inconsistencies
- Track data versioning
- Maintain data integrity across academic years

### CLI Design
- Provide clear, helpful command descriptions
- Implement progress bars for long operations
- Add verbose logging options
- Support different output formats
- Allow selective scraping of specific years/programs
- Implement dry-run options

### Database Operations
- Use transactions where appropriate
- Implement upsert operations
- Handle conflicts gracefully
- Maintain data consistency
- Track data modifications
- Implement rollback capabilities

### Error Handling
- Implement comprehensive error handling
- Log errors with appropriate context
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Allow for graceful degradation
- Support resume capabilities for failed operations

### Testing
- Unit tests for scraping logic
- Integration tests for database operations
- Mock network requests in tests
- Test different academic year formats
- Validate data integrity in tests

## CLI Commands

### Main Commands
```bash
# Scrape all data for a specific year
tmu-scraper scrape-year 2023

# Scrape specific program
tmu-scraper scrape-program "Computer Science"

# Update course data
tmu-scraper update-courses

# Validate scraped data
tmu-scraper validate

# Export data to JSON
tmu-scraper export --format json --output data/
```

### Options
- `--year`: Academic year to scrape
- `--program`: Specific program to scrape
- `--verbose`: Enable verbose logging
- `--dry-run`: Run without saving data
- `--force`: Force update existing data
- `--output`: Output directory for exports
- `--format`: Export format (json/csv)

